# The measure — the cap's derivation, and the parse the guard is keyed on

Everything here is read-only over the archive in the **main** tree (`.pi/issue-loop/items`), by `tools/loop-spend.py`
and the two counts below. Nothing writes to it.

## The population the reviewer's 81 is taken over

`tools/loop-spend.py` reads each item's `events.jsonl`, and each of its sessions carries the prompt it was started
with. For a reviewer that prompt is a file path — `<item>/reviewer-prompt-<round>[-restart].md` — so the session's
report is the file named after it, `<item>/reviewer-report-<round>[-restart].md`, and whether the session ended with a
verdict is a fact of that file, read as `report_lines` (`tools/loop/evidence.sh`) reads it: one backtick stripped at
each end, so a closing block written as inline code is still the line it states (#793).

    for each item in .pi/issue-loop/items/*/events.jsonl:
        for each session of read_sessions(path) whose role is "reviewer":
            turns  = len(session turns that carry usage)
            report = session prompt path with reviewer-prompt- -> reviewer-report-
            verdict_ended = the report has a line (after one backtick strip) that starts "VERDICT:"

$ python3 tools/loop-spend.py --items .pi/issue-loop/items      # the reader whose sessions this walks
$ (the walk above, `read_sessions` imported from tools/loop-spend.py: no other input)
sessions=65 with_verdict=37 without=28
at_110_all=20 at_110_verdict=2
verdict_ended: n=37 p25=74 median=81 p75=98 max=110
all: n=65 p50=90 p75=110 p95=110 max=110
days=2026-11-17..2026-11-25 items=41

`LOOP_CHILD_TURNS_MAX_REVIEWER` is therefore **81**: the median of the 37 sessions that reached a verdict, over the
65 reviewer sessions of 41 items from 2026-11-17 to 2026-11-25. The old 110 is where **20 of the 65** sit — a fifth of
the population ended at the ceiling, which is what a p95 taken over a sample censored there can only return.

The 37 verdict-ended turns, sorted, are the derivation in one line:

    28 46 50 51 66 67 70 72 74 74 75 75 75 77 77 78 79 80 81 81 86 87 89 90 92 94 95 98 100 100 102 104 105 106 106 110 110

so the median is the 19th of 37, `81`, and p25 is `74`. p75 reads `98` on the linear interpolation between the 98 and
the 100 that straddle it (the fix commit's message states `99`, the same boundary read with the other percentile
convention); the figure the key holds is the median, and the median is the same under either.

