## The parse the guard is keyed on, and the two reports that moved it

The guard asks whether the round **named** a finding, not what it claimed to have counted. The finding line is the
one thing a worker pass can act on, and a reviewer may put markdown around it; the archive writes it that way twice.
Read by the shipped `blocking_findings()` (`tools/loop/evidence.sh`, `grep -cE '^[[:space:]>*_]*BLOCKING:'` over
`report_lines`), beside the reviewed pattern `grep -c '^BLOCKING:'`:

$ here=tools/loop bash -c 'source tools/loop/evidence.sh; blocking_findings <report>; grep -c "^BLOCKING:" <report>'
/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/issue-loop/items/1042/reviewer-report-1-restart.md
  blocking_findings=2  line-start=0
/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/issue-loop/items/999/reviewer-report-2.md
  blocking_findings=1  line-start=0
/home/tim/projects/NaviVeylin/.pi/issue-loop/items/118/reviewer-report-1.md
  blocking_findings=3  line-start=3

`1042/reviewer-report-1-restart.md` states `LOOP-BLOCKING: 1`, `LOOP-CORRECTIONS-ONLY: yes`, `VERDICT: FAIL`, and
writes its one finding on lines 19 and 118 as `**BLOCKING: the delta's scenario … **`; the round after it
(`reviewer-report-2.md`) states `LOOP-BLOCKING: 0` / `VERDICT: PASS`. Read by the reviewed pattern the report names
nothing, the guard breaks before the corrective arm, the item is refused, and the note on it would say the round named
nothing — a round that in fact passed would never have been taken. `999/reviewer-report-2.md:38` writes its finding
the same way (`**BLOCKING: the gate measured a different tree than this diff.**`, `LOOP-BLOCKING: 1`).

## Every refusing report in the archive names a finding

$ (the walk over .pi/issue-loop/items/*/reviewer-report-*.md, verdict read as `report_lines` reads it)
report_files=83 failing=20
with_line_start=18 with_emphasis_only=2 with_none=0
fenced_blocking_lines=0
emphasis-only 1042/reviewer-report-1-restart.md emph=2
emphasis-only 999/reviewer-report-2.md emph=1

20 reports refuse, and every one of them carries a `BLOCKING:` line — 18 at line start, 2 wrapped in emphasis, **none**
with no finding at all. (`grep -l '^VERDICT: FAIL'` counts 19: the twentieth, `999/reviewer-report-1.md`, writes
`` `VERDICT: FAIL` `` as inline code, which the run reads as the verdict it states and a plain grep for the bare line
misses.) So the count a report states is not where the pointer is, and the line is read whether or not markdown wraps
it.

**The other direction, stated rather than hidden.** The parse does not skip fenced blocks, and a `BLOCKING:` line
quoted inside one would be counted as a finding. No report in the archive has one (`fenced_blocking_lines=0` above),
and the consequence of over-matching is a round spent, never a round skipped — the direction this guard must not fail
in. A quoted `> BLOCKING:` line is over-matched the same way, deliberately.
